1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:03,579
part 1 chapter 5.

2
00:00:03,579 --> 00:00:09,520
in the low ceiling canteen, deep underground the lunch queue jerked slowly forward.

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:14,319
The room was already very full and deafeningly noisy. From the Grill at the

4
00:00:14,319 --> 00:00:18,520
counter the steam of stew came pouring forth, with a sour metallic smell which

5
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,880
did not quite overcome the fumes of Victory Gin.

6
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:23,979
On the far side of the room there was a

7
00:00:23,979 --> 00:00:27,760
small bar, a mere hole in the wall, where gin could be bought at 10 cents the

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,100
large nip.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,119
'Just the man I was looking for,' said a voice at Winston's back.

10
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:38,979
He turned round. It was his friend Sim who worked in the Research Department.

11
00:00:38,979 --> 00:00:43,420
Perhaps 'friend' was not exactly the right word. You did not have friends nowadays,

12
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:49,359
you had comrades: but there were some comrades whose society was pleasanter than that of others

13
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:53,139
Simon was a philologist, a specialist in Newspeak.

14
00:00:53,139 --> 00:00:57,279
Indeed, he was one of the enormous team of experts now engaged in compiling the

15
00:00:57,279 --> 00:01:00,219
11th Edition of the Newspeak Dictionary.

16
00:01:00,219 --> 00:01:03,939
He was a tiny creature, smaller than Winston, with dark hair and large,

17
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:08,920
protuberant eyes, at once mournful and derisive, which seemed to search your

18
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:12,579
face closely while he was speaking to you.

19
00:01:12,579 --> 00:01:16,300
'I wanted to ask you whether you'd got any razor blades,' he said.

20
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:20,439
'Not one!' said Winston with a sort of guilty haste. 'I've tried all over the

21
00:01:20,439 --> 00:01:23,158
place. They don't exist any longer.'

22
00:01:23,158 --> 00:01:27,939
Everyone kept asking you for razor blades. Actually he had two unused ones

23
00:01:27,939 --> 00:01:29,439
which he was hoarding up.

24
00:01:29,439 --> 00:01:32,319
There had been a famine of them for months past.

25
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:35,920
At any given moment there was some necessary article which the Party shops

26
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:37,658
were unable to supply.

27
00:01:37,658 --> 00:01:41,738
Sometimes it was buttons, sometimes it was darning wool, sometimes it was

28
00:01:41,738 --> 00:01:44,560
shoelaces; at present it was razor blades.

29
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:48,760
You could only get hold of them, if at all, by scrounging more or less furtively

30
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:51,340
on the 'free' market.

31
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:56,079
'I've been using the same blade for six weeks,' he added untruthfully.

32
00:01:56,079 --> 00:01:58,420
The queue gave another jerk forward.

33
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,479
As they halted he turned and faced Sim again.

34
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:06,819
Each of them took a greasy metal tray from a pile at the end of the counter.

35
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:10,300
'Did you go and see the prisoners hanged yesterday?' said Sim

36
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,460
I was working,' said Winston indifferently.

37
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,520
'I shall see it on the flicks, I suppose.'

38
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:22,119
'A very inadequate substitute,' said Sim His mocking eyes roved over Winston's face.

39
00:02:22,119 --> 00:02:26,198
'I know you,' the eyes seem to say, 'I see through you.

40
00:02:26,198 --> 00:02:30,219
I know very well why you didn't go to see those prisoners hanged.'

41
00:02:30,219 --> 00:02:34,179
In an intellectual way Simon was venomously orthodox.

42
00:02:34,179 --> 00:02:37,779
He would talk with a disagreeable gloating satisfaction of helicopter

43
00:02:37,779 --> 00:02:42,219
raids on enemy villages and trials and confessions of thought criminals the

44
00:02:42,219 --> 00:02:45,158
executions and the sellers of the Ministry of Love.

45
00:02:45,158 --> 00:02:49,179
Talking to him was largely a matter of getting him away from such subjects and

46
00:02:49,179 --> 00:02:53,500
entangling him, if possible, in the technicalities of Newspeak, on which he

47
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:55,840
was authoritative and interesting.

48
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:01,779
Winston turned his head a little aside to avoid the scrutiny of the large dark eyes.

49
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:06,158
'It was a good hanging,' said same reminiscently. 'I think it spoils it when

50
00:03:06,158 --> 00:03:07,658
they tie their feet together.

51
00:03:07,658 --> 00:03:12,340
I like to see them kicking. And above all, at the end, the tongue sticking right out,

52
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:17,920
and blue quite a bright blue. That's the detail that appeals to me

53
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:22,719
next please yield the white apron with the ladle.

54
00:03:22,719 --> 00:03:27,100
Winston and Sim pushed their trays beneath the grill Onto each was dumped

55
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:32,198
swiftly the regulation lunch— —a metal panicking of pinkish gray stew, a hunk of

56
00:03:32,198 --> 00:03:39,039
bread, a cube of cheese, a mug of milkless Victory Coffee, and one saccharin tablet.

57
00:03:39,039 --> 00:03:44,979
'There's a table over there, under that telescreen,' said sign Let's pick up a gin on the way.'

58
00:03:44,979 --> 00:03:49,300
The gin was served out to them in handle this china mugs. They threaded their way

59
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:52,719
across the crowded room and unpacked their trays onto the metal topped table,

60
00:03:52,719 --> 00:03:57,279
on one corner of which someone had left a pool of stew, a filthy liquid mess that

61
00:03:57,279 --> 00:03:59,679
had the appearance of vomit.

62
00:03:59,679 --> 00:04:04,359
Winston took up his mug of gin, paused for an instant to collect his nerve, and

63
00:04:04,359 --> 00:04:07,179
gulped the oily tasting stuff down.

64
00:04:07,179 --> 00:04:12,340
When he had winked the tears out of his eyes he suddenly discovered that he was hungry.

65
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:16,300
He began swallowing spoonfuls of the stew, which, in among its general

66
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:22,479
sloppiness, had cubes of spongy pinkish stuff which was probably a preparation of meat.

67
00:04:22,479 --> 00:04:26,679
Neither of them spoke again till they had emptied their pannicans

68
00:04:26,679 --> 00:04:31,119
From the table at Winston's left, a little behind his back, someone was

69
00:04:31,119 --> 00:04:35,260
talking rapidly and continuously, a harsh gavel almost like the quacking of a duck,

70
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:39,520
which pierced the general uproar of the room.

71
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:44,260
'How is the Dictionary getting on?' said Winston, raising his voice to overcome the noise.

72
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:47,020
'Slowly,' said Sim I'm on the adjectives.

73
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,779
It's fascinating.'

74
00:04:49,779 --> 00:04:53,800
He had brightened up immediately at the mention of Newspeak. He pushed his

75
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:57,698
pannikin aside, took up his hunk of bread in one delicate hand and his cheese in

76
00:04:57,698 --> 00:05:03,039
the other, and leaned across the table so as to be able to speak without shouting.

77
00:05:03,039 --> 00:05:06,639
'The 11th Edition is the definitive edition,' he said. 'We're getting the

78
00:05:06,639 --> 00:05:11,439
language into its final shape— —the shape it's going to have when nobody speaks anything else.

79
00:05:11,439 --> 00:05:15,579
When we finished with it, people like you will have to learn it all over again.

80
00:05:15,579 --> 00:05:19,658
You think I'd dare say, that our chief job is inventing new words. But not a bit

81
00:05:19,658 --> 00:05:23,800
of it! We're destroying words— —scores of them, hundreds of them, every day.

82
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,819
We're cutting the language down to the bone. The 11th Edition won't contain a

83
00:05:27,819 --> 00:05:32,079
single word that will become obsolete before the year 2050.'.

84
00:05:32,079 --> 00:05:35,859
He bit hungrily into his bread and swallowed a couple of mouthfuls, then

85
00:05:35,859 --> 00:05:39,158
continued speaking, with a sort of patent's passion.

86
00:05:39,158 --> 00:05:43,119
His thin dark face had become animated, his eyes had lost their mocking

87
00:05:43,119 --> 00:05:45,600
expression and grown almost dreamy.

88
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:50,319
'It's a beautiful thing, the destruction of words. Of course the great wastage is

89
00:05:50,319 --> 00:05:53,619
in the verbs and adjectives, but there are hundreds of nouns that can be got

90
00:05:53,619 --> 00:05:55,119
rid of as well.

91
00:05:55,119 --> 00:05:59,979
It isn't only the synonyms; there are also the antonyms. After all, what

92
00:05:59,979 --> 00:06:03,880
justification is there for a word which is simply the opposite of some other word?

93
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:06,340
A word contains its opposite in itself.

94
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:10,899
Take "good", for instance. If you have a word like "good", what need is there for a

95
00:06:10,899 --> 00:06:12,219
word like "bad"?

96
00:06:12,219 --> 00:06:17,979
"Ungood" will do just as well— —better, because it's an exact opposite, which the other is not.

97
00:06:17,979 --> 00:06:21,698
Or again, if you want a stronger version of "good", what sense is there in having a

98
00:06:21,698 --> 00:06:26,319
whole string of vague useless words like "excellent" and "splendid" and all the rest of them

99
00:06:26,319 --> 00:06:31,238
pluscode covers the meaning; or double plus good if you want something stronger still.

100
00:06:31,238 --> 00:06:35,198
Of course we use those forms already, but in the final version of Newspeak

101
00:06:35,198 --> 00:06:37,000
there'll be nothing else.

102
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,600
In the end the whole notion of goodness and badness will be covered by only six

103
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:46,000
words— —in reality, only one word. Don't you see the beauty of that, Winston? It was

104
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,500
BB's idea originally, of course,' he added it as an afterthought.

105
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:55,300
A sort of vapodeagonist flitted across Winston's face at the mention of Big Brother.

106
00:06:55,300 --> 00:07:00,399
Nevertheless I'm immediately detected a certain lack of enthusiasm.

107
00:07:00,399 --> 00:07:05,319
'You haven't a real appreciation of new speak Winston,' he said almost sadly. 'Even

108
00:07:05,319 --> 00:07:09,880
when you write it you're still thinking in Old speak I've read some of those pieces that you

109
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:14,560
write in the Times occasionally. They're good enough, but they're translations.

110
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:18,340
In your heart you'd prefer to stick to Old speak with all its vagueness and its

111
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:20,260
useless shades of meaning.

112
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,500
You don't grasp the beauty of the destruction of words.

113
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:27,100
Do you know that Newspeak is the only language in the world whose vocabulary

114
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:29,679
gets smaller every year?'

115
00:07:29,679 --> 00:07:33,880
Winston did know that, of course. He smiled, sympathetically he hoped, not

116
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:37,840
trusting himself to speak sign bit off another fragment of the

117
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:43,179
dark colored bread Jude it briefly, and went on: 'Don't you see that the whole aim of news

118
00:07:43,179 --> 00:07:45,639
speakers to narrow the range of thought?

119
00:07:45,639 --> 00:07:49,479
In the end we shall make thought crime literally impossible, because there will

120
00:07:49,479 --> 00:07:51,520
be no words in which to express it.

121
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,600
Every concept that can ever be needed will be expressed by exactly one word,

122
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:01,359
with its meaning rigidly defined and all its subsidiary meanings rubbed out and forgotten.

123
00:08:01,359 --> 00:08:04,899
Already, in the 11th Edition, we're not far from that point.

124
00:08:04,899 --> 00:08:08,800
But the process will still be continuing long after you and I are dead.

125
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:14,319
Every year fewer and fewer words, and the range of consciousness always a little smaller.

126
00:08:14,319 --> 00:08:18,340
Even now, of course, there's no reason or excuse for committing thought crime It's

127
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:23,139
merely a question of self-discipline reality control But in the end there won't be any need

128
00:08:23,139 --> 00:08:24,340
even for that.

129
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:28,899
The Revolution will be complete when the language is perfect news speak isingsoc

130
00:08:28,899 --> 00:08:33,639
and ing sock is Newspeak,' he added with a sort of mystical satisfaction. Has it

131
00:08:33,639 --> 00:08:38,260
ever occurred to you, Winston, that by the year 2050, at the very latest, not a

132
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:43,779
single human being will be alive who could understand such a conversation as we are having now?'

133
00:08:43,779 --> 00:08:47,800
'Except———' began Winston doubtfully, and he stopped.

134
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:51,698
It had been on the tip of his tongue to say 'Except the proles,' but he checked

135
00:08:51,698 --> 00:08:56,738
himself, not feeling fully certain that this remark was not in some way unorthodox

136
00:08:56,738 --> 00:09:00,579
same however, had defined what he was about to say.

137
00:09:00,579 --> 00:09:06,460
'The proles are not human beings,' he said carelessly. 'By 2050— —earlier, probably— —all

138
00:09:06,460 --> 00:09:10,119
real knowledge of old speak will have disappeared. The whole literature of the

139
00:09:10,119 --> 00:09:15,158
past will have been destroyed. Chaucer, Shakespeare, Milton, Byron— —they'll exist

140
00:09:15,158 --> 00:09:18,819
only in Newspeak versions, not merely changed into something different, but

141
00:09:18,819 --> 00:09:22,420
actually changed into something contradictory of what they used to be.

142
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:25,179
Even the literature of the Party will change.

143
00:09:25,179 --> 00:09:29,380
Even the slogans will change. How could you have a slogan like "freedom is

144
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:33,158
slavery" when the concept of freedom has been abolished?

145
00:09:33,158 --> 00:09:35,619
The whole climate of thought will be different.

146
00:09:35,619 --> 00:09:39,340
In fact there will be no thought, as we understand it now.

147
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:46,479
Orthodoxy means not thinking— —not needing to think. Orthodoxy is unconsciousness.'

148
00:09:46,479 --> 00:09:50,260
One of these days, thought Winston with sudden deep conviction Sam will be

149
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:55,600
vaporized. He is too intelligent. He sees too clearly and speaks too plainly.

150
00:09:55,600 --> 00:10:01,840
The Party does not like such people. One day he will disappear. It is written in his face.

151
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:05,979
Winston had finished his bread and cheese. He turned a little sideways in

152
00:10:05,979 --> 00:10:08,260
his chair to drink his mug of coffee.

153
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:13,719
At the table on his left the man with a strident voice was still talking remorselessly away.

154
00:10:13,719 --> 00:10:16,840
A young woman who was perhaps his secretary, and who was sitting with her

155
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:20,439
back to Winston, was listening to him and seemed to be eagerly agreeing with

156
00:10:20,439 --> 00:10:22,420
everything that he said.

157
00:10:22,420 --> 00:10:26,859
From time to time Winston caught some such remarkers I think you're so right. I

158
00:10:26,859 --> 00:10:31,479
do so agree with you', uttered in a youthful and rather silly feminine voice.

159
00:10:31,479 --> 00:10:35,619
But the other voice never stopped for an instant, even when the girl was speaking.

160
00:10:35,619 --> 00:10:39,939
Winston knew the man by sight, though he knew no more about him than that he held

161
00:10:39,939 --> 00:10:42,639
some important post in the Fiction Department.

162
00:10:42,639 --> 00:10:47,198
He was a man of about 30 with a muscular throat and a large, mobile mouth.

163
00:10:47,198 --> 00:10:50,738
His head was thrown back a little, and because of the angle at which he was

164
00:10:50,738 --> 00:10:54,698
sitting, his spectacles caught the light and presented to Winston two black discs

165
00:10:54,698 --> 00:10:56,439
instead of eyes.

166
00:10:56,439 --> 00:11:00,279
What was slightly horrible was that from the stream of sound that poured out of

167
00:11:00,279 --> 00:11:04,779
his mouth, it was almost impossible to distinguish a single word.

168
00:11:04,779 --> 00:11:08,859
Just once Winston quarter phrase—' —'complete and final elimination of

169
00:11:08,859 --> 00:11:13,899
Goldsteinism'— '—jerked out very rapidly and, as it seemed, all in one piece, like a

170
00:11:13,899 --> 00:11:16,000
line of type cast solid.

171
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,779
For the rest it was just a noise, a quack quack quacking

172
00:11:19,779 --> 00:11:23,738
And yet, though you could not actually hear what the man was saying, you could

173
00:11:23,738 --> 00:11:26,619
not be in any doubt about its general nature.

174
00:11:26,619 --> 00:11:30,158
He might be denouncing Goldstein and a demanding sterner measures against

175
00:11:30,158 --> 00:11:34,719
thought criminals and saboteurs, he might be fulminating against the atrocities of

176
00:11:34,719 --> 00:11:38,500
the Eurasian army, he might be praising Big Brother or the heroes on the Malabar

177
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,840
front— —it made no difference.

178
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:46,840
Whatever it was, you could be certain that every word of it was pure orthodoxy, pure insock

179
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:51,340
As he watched the eyeless face with the jaw moving rapidly up and down, Winston

180
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:55,840
had a curious feeling that this was not a real human being but some kind of dummy.

181
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:59,260
It was not the man's brain that was speaking, it was his larynx.

182
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:03,219
The stuff that was coming out of him consisted of words, but it was not speech

183
00:12:03,219 --> 00:12:09,579
in the true sense: it was a noise uttered in unconsciousness, like the quacking of a duck

184
00:12:09,579 --> 00:12:13,960
Simon had fallen silent for a moment, and with the handle if his spoon was tracing

185
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:16,179
patterns in the puddle of stew.

186
00:12:16,179 --> 00:12:20,020
The voice from the other table quacked rapidly on, easily audible in spite of

187
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:22,779
the surrounding din.

188
00:12:22,779 --> 00:12:27,100
'There is a word in Newspeak,' said Sim I don't know whether you know it

189
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:29,560
duck speak to quack like a duck.

190
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:33,399
It is one of those interesting words that have two contradictory meanings.

191
00:12:33,399 --> 00:12:39,600
Applied to an opponent, it is abuse; applied to someone you agree with, it is praise.'

192
00:12:39,779 --> 00:12:43,300
Unquestionably sign will be vaporized, Winston thought again.

193
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:47,020
He thought it with a kind of sadness, although well knowing that Simon

194
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,859
despised him and slightly disliked him, and was fully capable of denouncing him

195
00:12:50,859 --> 00:12:53,979
as a thought criminal if he saw any reason for doing so.

196
00:12:53,979 --> 00:12:58,198
There was something subtly wrong with Sim There was something that he lacked:

197
00:12:58,198 --> 00:13:01,960
discretion, aloofness, a sort of saving stupidity.

198
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,880
You could not say that he was unorthodox.

199
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:08,198
He believed in the principles of exoc he venerated Big Brother, he rejoiced over

200
00:13:08,198 --> 00:13:12,760
victories, he hated heretics, not merely with sincerity but with a sort of

201
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:16,840
restless zeal, an up-to-dateness of information, which the ordinary Party

202
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:22,779
member did not approach. Yet a faint air of disreputability always clung to him.

203
00:13:22,779 --> 00:13:26,979
He said things that would have been better unsaid, he had read too many books,

204
00:13:26,979 --> 00:13:31,539
he frequented the Chestnut Tree Cafe haunt of painters and musicians.

205
00:13:31,539 --> 00:13:35,139
There was no law, not even an unwritten law, against frequenting the Chestnut

206
00:13:35,139 --> 00:13:40,420
Tree Cafe yet the place was somehow illumined The old, discredited leaders of

207
00:13:40,420 --> 00:13:44,260
the Party had been used to gather there before they were finally purged.

208
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:49,600
Goldstein himself, it was said, had sometimes been seen there, years and decades ago

209
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:52,600
Sam's fate was not difficult to foresee.

210
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:56,260
And yet it was a fact that if Simon grasped, even for three seconds, the

211
00:13:56,260 --> 00:14:01,600
nature of his, Winston's, secret opinions, he would betray him instantly to the Thought Police.

212
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:05,439
So would anybody else, for that matter: but sign more than most.

213
00:14:05,439 --> 00:14:14,319
Zeal was not enough. Orthodoxy was unconsciousness Sim looked up. 'Here comes Parsons,' he said.

214
00:14:14,319 --> 00:14:18,340
Something in the tone of his voice seemed to add, 'that bloody fool.'

215
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:22,600
Parsons, Winston's fellow tenant at Victory Mansions, was in fact threading

216
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:28,060
his way across the room— —a tubby, middle-sized man with fair hair and a frog-like face.

217
00:14:28,060 --> 00:14:32,738
At 35 he was already putting on rolls of fat at neck and waistline, but his

218
00:14:32,738 --> 00:14:34,779
movements were brisk and boyish.

219
00:14:34,779 --> 00:14:38,979
His whole appearance was that of a little boy grown large, so much so that

220
00:14:38,979 --> 00:14:42,939
although he was wearing the regulation overalls, it was almost impossible not to

221
00:14:42,939 --> 00:14:48,880
think of him as being dressed in the blue shorts gray shirt and rednecker chief of the Spies.

222
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:52,840
In visualizing him one always saw a picture of dimpled knees and sleeves

223
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:55,420
rolled back from pudgy forearms.

224
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:59,800
Parsons did, indeed, invariably revert to shorts when a community hike or any

225
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,979
other physical activity gave him an excuse for doing so.

226
00:15:02,979 --> 00:15:07,899
He greeted them both with a cheery hello hello and sat down at the table, giving

227
00:15:07,899 --> 00:15:10,179
off an intense smell of sweat.

228
00:15:10,179 --> 00:15:15,819
Beads of moisture stood out all over his pink face. His powers of sweating were extraordinary.

229
00:15:15,819 --> 00:15:19,060
At the Community center you could always tell when he had been playing table

230
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:22,118
tennis by the dampness of the bat handle.

231
00:15:22,118 --> 00:15:25,658
Syme had produced a strip of paper on which there was a long column of words,

232
00:15:25,658 --> 00:15:30,100
and was studying it with an ink pencil between his fingers.

233
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:35,800
'Look at him working away in the lunch hour,' said Parsons, nudging Winston Keen to say

234
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:37,359
What's that you've got there, old boy?

235
00:15:37,359 --> 00:15:40,420
Something a bit too brainy for me, I expect.

236
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:46,600
Smith, old boy, I'll tell you why I'm chasing you. It's that sub you forgot to give me.'

237
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:50,439
'Which sub is that?' said Winston, automatically feeling for money.

238
00:15:50,439 --> 00:15:54,100
About a quarter of one's salary had to be earmarked for voluntary subscriptions,

239
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:58,359
which were so numerous that it was difficult to keep track of them.

240
00:15:58,359 --> 00:16:03,760
'For Hate Week. You know— —the house by house fund. I'm treasurer for our block.

241
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:08,618
We're making an all-out effort— —going to put on a tremendous show. I tell you, it

242
00:16:08,618 --> 00:16:11,618
won't be my fault if old Victory Mansions doesn't have the biggest outfit

243
00:16:11,618 --> 00:16:13,600
of flags in the whole street.

244
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:15,939
Two dollars you promised me.'

245
00:16:15,939 --> 00:16:19,779
Winston found and handed over two creased and filthy notes, which Parsons

246
00:16:19,779 --> 00:16:24,158
entered in a small notebook, in the neat handwriting of the illiterate.

247
00:16:24,158 --> 00:16:28,359
'By the way, old boy,' he said. 'I hear that little beggar of mine let fly at you

248
00:16:28,359 --> 00:16:29,920
with his catapult yesterday.

249
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:32,079
I gave him a good dressing down for it.

250
00:16:32,079 --> 00:16:35,979
In fact I told him I'd take the catapult away if he does it again.'

251
00:16:35,979 --> 00:16:39,939
'I think he was a little upset at not going to the execution,' said Winston.

252
00:16:39,939 --> 00:16:44,738
'Ah, well— —what I mean to say that shows the right spirit, doesn't it?

253
00:16:44,738 --> 00:16:48,760
Mischievous little big as they are, both of them, but talk about keenness!

254
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:52,359
All they think about is the Spies, and the war, of course

255
00:16:52,359 --> 00:16:55,779
do you know what that little girl of mine did last Saturday, when her troop

256
00:16:55,779 --> 00:16:58,060
was on a high card birkhamsted way?

257
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:01,899
She got two other girls to go with her, slipped off from the hike and spent the

258
00:17:01,899 --> 00:17:04,238
whole afternoon following a strange man.

259
00:17:04,238 --> 00:17:07,899
They kept on his tail for two hours, right through the woods, and then, when

260
00:17:07,899 --> 00:17:11,198
they got into amisham handed him over to the patrols.'

261
00:17:11,198 --> 00:17:15,279
'What did they do that for?' said Winston, somewhat taken aback.

262
00:17:15,279 --> 00:17:19,658
Parsons went on triumphantly: 'My kid made sure he was some kind of

263
00:17:19,658 --> 00:17:25,000
enemy agent— —might have been dropped by parachute, for instance. But here's the point oh boy.

264
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,760
What do you think put her on to him in the first place?

265
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:32,020
She spotted he was wearing a funny kind of shoes— —said she'd never seen anyone

266
00:17:32,020 --> 00:17:33,819
wearing shoes like that before.

267
00:17:33,819 --> 00:17:36,039
So the chances were he was a foreigner.

268
00:17:36,039 --> 00:17:39,399
Pretty smart for a nipper of seven, eh?'

269
00:17:39,399 --> 00:17:44,198
'What happened to the man?' said Winston oh that I couldn't say, of course. But I

270
00:17:44,198 --> 00:17:48,039
wouldn't be altogether surprised if———' Parsons made the motion of aiming a

271
00:17:48,039 --> 00:17:51,639
rifle, and clicked his tongue for the explosion.

272
00:17:51,639 --> 00:17:56,560
'Good,' said Sam abstractedly, without looking up from his strip of paper.

273
00:17:56,560 --> 00:18:00,639
'Of course we can't afford to take chances,' agreed Winston dutifully.

274
00:18:00,639 --> 00:18:04,600
'What I mean to say, there is a war on,' said Parsons.

275
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:08,020
As though in confirmation of this, a trumpet call floated from the telescreen

276
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:09,939
just above their heads.

277
00:18:09,939 --> 00:18:14,198
However, it was not the proclamation of a military victory this time, but merely an

278
00:18:14,198 --> 00:18:16,960
announcement from the Ministry of Plenty.

279
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:19,238
'Comrades!' cried an eager youthful voice.

280
00:18:19,238 --> 00:18:23,859
'Attention, comrades! We have glorious news for you. We have won the battle for

281
00:18:23,859 --> 00:18:28,060
production! Returns now completed of the output of all classes of consumption

282
00:18:28,060 --> 00:18:32,020
goods show that the standard of living has risen by no less than 20 percent

283
00:18:32,020 --> 00:18:34,000
over the past year.

284
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,139
All over Oceania this morning there were irrepressible spontaneous demonstrations

285
00:18:38,139 --> 00:18:41,979
when workers marched out of factories and offices and paraded through the

286
00:18:41,979 --> 00:18:45,399
streets with banners voicing their gratitude to Big Brother for the new,

287
00:18:45,399 --> 00:18:48,880
happy life which his wise leadership has bestowed upon us.

288
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:51,158
Here are some of the completed figures.

289
00:18:51,158 --> 00:18:57,939
Foodstuffs— The phrase 'our new, happy life' recurred several times. It had been a favorite of

290
00:18:57,939 --> 00:19:00,039
late with the Ministry of Plenty.

291
00:19:00,039 --> 00:19:04,238
Parsons, his attention caught by the trumpets call, sat listening with a sort

292
00:19:04,238 --> 00:19:07,779
of gaping solemnity, a sort of edified boredom.

293
00:19:07,779 --> 00:19:10,960
He could not follow the figures, but he was aware that they were in some way a

294
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:12,760
cause for satisfaction.

295
00:19:12,760 --> 00:19:17,979
He had lugged out a huge and filthy pipe which was already half full of charred tobacco.

296
00:19:17,979 --> 00:19:22,238
With the tobacco ration at 100 grams a week it was seldom possible to fill a

297
00:19:22,238 --> 00:19:23,679
pipe to the top.

298
00:19:23,679 --> 00:19:28,179
Winston was smoking a Victory Cigarette which he held carefully horizontal.

299
00:19:28,179 --> 00:19:32,560
The new ration did not start till tomorrow and he had only four cigarettes left.

300
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:36,100
For the moment he had shut his ears to the remoter noises and was listening to

301
00:19:36,100 --> 00:19:38,920
the stuff that streamed out of the telescreen.

302
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:42,579
It appeared that there had even been demonstrations to thank Big Brother for

303
00:19:42,579 --> 00:19:45,579
raising the chocolate ration to 20 grams a week.

304
00:19:45,579 --> 00:19:49,600
And only yesterday, he reflected, it had been announced that the ration was to be

305
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,880
reduced to 20 grams a week.

306
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:56,319
Was it possible that they could swallow that, after only 24 hours?

307
00:19:56,319 --> 00:20:01,600
Yes, they swallowed it. Parsons swallowed it easily, with the stupidity of an animal.

308
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:05,198
The eyeless creature at the other table swallowed it fanatically, passionately,

309
00:20:05,198 --> 00:20:10,060
with a furious desire to track down, denounce and vaporize anyone who should

310
00:20:10,060 --> 00:20:16,420
suggest that last week the ration had been 30 grams slime too— —in some more complex way,

311
00:20:16,420 --> 00:20:19,238
involving double think Sam swallowed it.

312
00:20:19,238 --> 00:20:23,319
Was he, then, alone in the possession of a memory?

313
00:20:23,319 --> 00:20:28,118
The fabulous statistics continued to pour out of the telescreen. As compared

314
00:20:28,118 --> 00:20:32,979
with last year there was more food, more clothes, more houses, more furniture, more

315
00:20:32,979 --> 00:20:38,679
cooking pots more fuel, more ships, more helicopters, more books, more babies— —more

316
00:20:38,679 --> 00:20:41,800
of everything except disease, crime and insanity.

317
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:47,920
Year by year and minute by minute, everybody and everything was whizzing rapidly upwards.

318
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:52,238
As Simon had done earlier. Winston had taken up his spoon and was dabbling in

319
00:20:52,238 --> 00:20:56,260
the pale colored gravy that dribbled across the table, drawing a long streak

320
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:58,118
of it out into a pattern.

321
00:20:58,118 --> 00:21:01,600
He meditated resentfully on the physical texture of life.

322
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:06,039
Had it always been like this? Had food always tasted like this?

323
00:21:06,039 --> 00:21:08,139
He looked around the canteen.

324
00:21:08,139 --> 00:21:12,819
A low ceiling crowded room, its walls grimy from the contact of innumerable

325
00:21:12,819 --> 00:21:17,920
bodies; battered metal tables and chairs, placed so close together that you sat

326
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:22,420
with elbows touching Ben spoons, dented trays, coarse white

327
00:21:22,420 --> 00:21:28,179
mugs; all surfaces greasy, grime in every crack; and a sourish, composite smell of

328
00:21:28,179 --> 00:21:32,500
bad gin and bad coffee in the metallic stew and dirty clothes.

329
00:21:32,500 --> 00:21:36,279
Always in your stomach and in your skin there was a sort of protest,

330
00:21:36,279 --> 00:21:39,760
a feeling that you had been cheated of something that you had a right to.

331
00:21:39,760 --> 00:21:43,600
It was true that he had no memories of anything greatly different.

332
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:47,020
In any time that he could accurately remember, there had never been quite

333
00:21:47,020 --> 00:21:50,500
enough to eat, one had never had socks or under clothes that were not full of

334
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:55,840
holes, furniture had always been battered and rickety, rooms underheated, tube

335
00:21:55,840 --> 00:22:01,658
drains crowded, houses falling to pieces red dark colored tear rarity, coffee

336
00:22:01,658 --> 00:22:07,840
filthy tasting cigarettes insufficient— —nothing cheap and plentiful except synthetic gin.

337
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:12,219
And though, of course, it grew worse as one's body aged, was it not a sign that

338
00:22:12,219 --> 00:22:15,760
this was not the natural order of things, if one's heart sickened at the

339
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:20,260
discomfort and dirt and scarcity, the interminable winters, the stickiness of

340
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:25,179
one's socks, the lifts that never worked, the cold water, the gritty soap, the

341
00:22:25,179 --> 00:22:29,380
cigarettes that came to pieces, the food with its strange evil tastes?

342
00:22:29,380 --> 00:22:33,279
Why should one feel it to be intolerable unless one had some kind of ancestral

343
00:22:33,279 --> 00:22:36,340
memory that things had once been different?

344
00:22:36,340 --> 00:22:38,520
He looked around the canteen again.

345
00:22:38,520 --> 00:22:42,880
Nearly everyone was ugly, and would still have been ugly even if dressed otherwise

346
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:45,698
than in the uniform blue overalls.

347
00:22:45,698 --> 00:22:50,198
On the far side of the room, sitting at a table alone, a small, curiously

348
00:22:50,198 --> 00:22:54,219
beetle-like man was drinking a cup of coffee, his little eyes darting

349
00:22:54,219 --> 00:22:56,800
suspicious glances from side to side.

350
00:22:56,800 --> 00:23:00,880
How easy it was, thought Winston, if you did not look about you, to believe that

351
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:06,279
the physical type set up by the Party is an ideal— —tall muscular youths and deep bosomed

352
00:23:06,279 --> 00:23:12,760
maidens blonde-haired vital, sunburnt, carefree— existed and even predominated.

353
00:23:12,760 --> 00:23:16,779
Actually, so far as he could judge, the majority of people in Airstrip One were

354
00:23:16,779 --> 00:23:21,100
small, dark and ill-favored It was curious how that beetle-like type

355
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:25,179
proliferated in the Ministries: little dumpy men, growing start very

356
00:23:25,179 --> 00:23:30,039
early in life, with short legs, swift scuttling movements, and fat inscrutable

357
00:23:30,039 --> 00:23:34,300
faces with very small eyes. It was the type that seemed to flourish best under

358
00:23:34,300 --> 00:23:36,819
the domination of the Party.

359
00:23:36,819 --> 00:23:40,300
The announcement from the Ministry of Plenty ended on another trumpet call and

360
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:42,279
gave way to tinny music.

361
00:23:42,279 --> 00:23:47,020
Parsons, stirred to vague enthusiasm by the bombardment of figures, took his pipe

362
00:23:47,020 --> 00:23:48,698
out of his mouth.

363
00:23:48,698 --> 00:23:52,359
'The Ministry of Plenty certainly done a good job this year,' he said with a

364
00:23:52,359 --> 00:23:53,920
knowing shake of his head.

365
00:23:53,920 --> 00:23:59,439
'By the way, Smith Oldboy I suppose you haven't got any razor blades you can let me have?'

366
00:23:59,439 --> 00:24:04,359
'Not one,' said Winston. 'I've been using the same blade for six weeks myself

367
00:24:04,359 --> 00:24:07,840
oh well— —just thought I'd ask you, old boy.'

368
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:10,600
'Sorry,' said Winston.

369
00:24:10,600 --> 00:24:14,679
The quacking voice from the next table, temporarily silenced during the

370
00:24:14,679 --> 00:24:18,520
Ministry's announcement, had started up again, as loud as ever.

371
00:24:18,520 --> 00:24:22,840
For some reason Winston suddenly found himself thinking of Mrs Parsons, with her

372
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:26,319
wispy hair and the dust in the creases of her face.

373
00:24:26,319 --> 00:24:30,340
Within two years those children would be denouncing her to the Thought Police.

374
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:35,380
Mrs Parsons would be vaporized sign would be vaporized. Winston would be

375
00:24:35,380 --> 00:24:38,260
vaporized. O'Brien would be vaporized.

376
00:24:38,260 --> 00:24:42,279
Parsons, on the other hand, would never be vaporized. The eyeless creature with the

377
00:24:42,279 --> 00:24:44,079
quacking voice would never be vaporized.

378
00:24:44,079 --> 00:24:48,579
The little beetle-like men who scuttled so nimbly through the Labyrinthian

379
00:24:48,579 --> 00:24:52,658
corridors of Ministries— —they, too, would never be vaporized.

380
00:24:52,658 --> 00:24:56,800
And the girl with dark hair, the girl from the Fiction Department— —she would

381
00:24:56,800 --> 00:24:58,779
never be vaporized either.

382
00:24:58,779 --> 00:25:03,219
It seems to him that he knew instinctively who would survive and who would perish

383
00:25:03,219 --> 00:25:08,520
so just what it was that made for survival, it was not easy to say.

384
00:25:08,618 --> 00:25:13,000
At this moment he was dragged out of his reverie with a violent jerk. The girl at

385
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,300
the next table had turned partly round and was looking at him.

386
00:25:16,300 --> 00:25:18,399
It was the girl with dark hair.

387
00:25:18,399 --> 00:25:23,020
She was looking at him in a side long way, but with curious intensity. The

388
00:25:23,020 --> 00:25:26,439
instant she caught his eye she looked away again.

389
00:25:26,439 --> 00:25:31,000
The sweat started out on Winston's backbone. A horrible pang of terror went

390
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:36,639
through him. It was gone almost at once, but it left a sort of nagging uneasiness behind.

391
00:25:36,639 --> 00:25:38,679
Why was she watching him?

392
00:25:38,679 --> 00:25:41,020
Why did she keep following him about?

393
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:44,500
Unfortunately he could not remember whether she had already been at the

394
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:47,738
table when he arrived, or had come there afterwards.

395
00:25:47,738 --> 00:25:51,579
But yesterday, at any rate, during the Two Minutes Hate, she had sat immediately

396
00:25:51,579 --> 00:25:55,060
behind him when there was no apparent need to do so.

397
00:25:55,060 --> 00:25:59,079
Quite likely her real object had been to listen to him and make sure whether he

398
00:25:59,079 --> 00:26:01,779
was shouting loudly enough.

399
00:26:01,779 --> 00:26:05,738
His earlier thought returned to him: probably she was not actually a member

400
00:26:05,738 --> 00:26:09,579
of the Thought Police, but then it was precisely the amateur spy who was the

401
00:26:09,579 --> 00:26:11,319
greatest danger of all.

402
00:26:11,319 --> 00:26:15,460
He did not know how long she had been looking at him, but perhaps for as much

403
00:26:15,460 --> 00:26:20,920
as five minutes, and it was possible that his features had not been perfectly under control.

404
00:26:20,920 --> 00:26:24,158
It was terribly dangerous to let your thoughts wonder when you're in any

405
00:26:24,158 --> 00:26:27,698
public place or within range of a telescreen.

406
00:26:27,698 --> 00:26:30,279
The smallest thing could give you away.

407
00:26:30,279 --> 00:26:35,439
A nervous tick an unconscious look of anxiety, a habit of muttering to yourself—

408
00:26:35,439 --> 00:26:40,779
anything that carried with it the suggestion of abnormality, of having something to hide.

409
00:26:40,779 --> 00:26:44,260
In any case, to wear an improper expression on your face (to look

410
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:49,179
incredulous when a victory was announced, for example) was itself a punishable offense

411
00:26:49,179 --> 00:26:54,819
There was even a word for it in Newspeak face crime it was called.

412
00:26:54,819 --> 00:26:58,539
The girl had turned her back on him again. Perhaps after all she was not

413
00:26:58,539 --> 00:27:02,139
really following him about; perhaps it was coincidence that she had sat so

414
00:27:02,139 --> 00:27:04,539
close to him two days running.

415
00:27:04,539 --> 00:27:08,618
His cigarette had gone out, and he laid it carefully on the edge of the table. He

416
00:27:08,618 --> 00:27:12,579
would finish smoking it after work, if he could keep the tobacco in it.

417
00:27:12,579 --> 00:27:16,060
Quite likely the person at the next table was a spy of the Thought Police,

418
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:19,539
and quite likely he would be in the cellars of the Ministry of Love within

419
00:27:19,539 --> 00:27:23,260
three days, but a cigarette end must not be wasted

420
00:27:23,260 --> 00:27:28,060
Sim had folded up his strip of paper and stowed it away in his pocket. Parsons had

421
00:27:28,060 --> 00:27:29,738
begun talking again.

422
00:27:29,738 --> 00:27:33,399
'Did I ever tell you, old boy,' he said, chuckling around the stem of his pipe,

423
00:27:33,399 --> 00:27:37,238
'about the time when those two nippers of mine set fire to the old Market woman's

424
00:27:37,238 --> 00:27:41,079
skirt because they saw her wrapping up sausages in a poster of bb

425
00:27:41,079 --> 00:27:45,399
Sneaked up behind her and set fire to it with a box of matches. Burned her quite

426
00:27:45,399 --> 00:27:50,738
badly, I believe. Little beggars, eh? But keen as mustard! That's a first-rate

427
00:27:50,738 --> 00:27:54,880
training they give them in the Spies nowadays— —better than in my day, even.

428
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:58,179
What do you think is the latest thing they've served them out with? Ear

429
00:27:58,179 --> 00:28:00,399
trumpets for listening through keyholes!

430
00:28:00,399 --> 00:28:04,300
My little girl brought one home the other night— —tried it out on our sitting

431
00:28:04,300 --> 00:28:07,960
room door, and reckoned she could hear twice as much as with her ear to the hole.

432
00:28:07,960 --> 00:28:13,359
Of course it's only a toy, mind you. Still, gives them the right idea, eh?'

433
00:28:13,359 --> 00:28:18,819
At this moment the telescreen let out a piercing whistle. It was a signal to return to work.

434
00:28:18,819 --> 00:28:22,420
All three men sprang to their feet to join in the struggle around the lifts,

435
00:28:22,420 --> 00:28:26,000
and the remaining tobacco fell out of Winston's cigarette
